WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.440
Amphibian goddesses, talking vegetables, and
nuclear bugs, oh my!

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:05.440
The Shazam!

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.800
comic books have had quite a bizarre and colorful
history.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:14.299
Back in the 1940s, comic book stories rarely
took up more than 10 pages, and serials were

00:00:14.299 --> 00:00:15.145
even rarer.

00:00:15.145 --> 00:00:20.900
But then there was the two-year marathon "Monster
Society of Evil" arc by Otto Binder and C.C.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.650
Beck in Captain Marvel Adventures 22 through
46.

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:30.740
The Monster Society is an interplanetary cabal
run by an unseen mastermind named Mr. Mind.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:36.070
He has the entire resources of the Axis Powers
at his disposal; even Hitler himself happily

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:37.350
takes orders.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:42.280
After months of nearly dying at Mr. Mind's
hands, Captain Marvel finds out that he's

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.960
actually a caterpillar with cute little glasses.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:50.160
But this doesn't make him any less of a threat,
as Mr. Mind turns the Great Wall of China

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.670
into a giant shield for the invading Japanese
army.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:59.590
This is a story so jam-packed that it introduces
two unrelated underground civilizations in

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:01.730
one five-page chapter.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:06.840
Other highlights include Mr. Mind firing so
many German cannons it knocks the Earth out

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:12.670
of orbit and invading Scotland in an island-shaped
battleship, as well as Captain Marvel on the

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:15.000
back of an unfrozen mammoth.

00:01:15.223 --> 00:01:21.096
Captain Marvel is many things, but we never
expected "dirty scab" to be part of his resume.

00:01:21.096 --> 00:01:23.770
But in "Captain Marvel and the Revolt of the
Comics!"

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:29.220
in Captain Marvel Adventures #22, that's pretty
much exactly what he is.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.420
Apparently, in the Captain Marvel universe,
comics heroes write and draw their own stories.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:38.909
That becomes a problem when stand-ins for
familiar characters all walk out, including

00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:40.770
Popeye and Flash Gordon.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:45.540
There's also a version of Batman who wears
a pair of baseball bats and has an actual

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.275
robin for his sidekick.

00:01:47.275 --> 00:01:48.510
"Holy headache."

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:52.740
Whatever their grievances might be, Captain
Marvel puts them back to work, whether by

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:57.710
appealing to their patriotism or by rescuing
his fellow superheroes from a danger even

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:01.050
they can't handle: a mob at the department
store.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:06.500
Some of the over-the-top flag-waving in wartime
comics can seem a little ridiculous.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.334
But that's not true for every story.

00:02:08.334 --> 00:02:10.873
Some of them are very ridiculous.

00:02:10.873 --> 00:02:15.730
For instance, there's "Captain Marvel Visits
Portland, Oregon, or Knighthood Flowers Again!"

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.480
in Captain Marvel Adventures #29.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.379
Cap is promoting a scrap metal drive for the
war effort when he meets with a modern-day

00:02:22.379 --> 00:02:25.120
Don Quixote named Richard Chickenheart.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.480
Chickenheart's obsession with artifacts has
led him to regular "spells" in which he thinks

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:30.980
he's a medieval knight.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:35.430
To keep him out of trouble, Billy Batson tags
along as his pageboy in between turning into

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:39.760
Captain Marvel so that he can intervene when
Chickenheart becomes violent.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:43.670
Before his mental breakdown, Chickenheart
is about to melt down his priceless collection

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:47.129
of unique historical treasures to make into
guns and tanks.

00:02:47.129 --> 00:02:51.790
All his troubles turn out to be the fault
of another collector named Bart Black, who

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:56.909
wants to buy Chickenheart's collection for
$5,000, which somehow makes him the bad guy.

00:02:56.909 --> 00:03:00.769
But nobody seems to realize that all that
money could pay for a lot more military gear

00:03:00.769 --> 00:03:04.226
than anyone could ever get from a couple pounds
of armor.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:09.690
In Captain Marvel's comics, natural phenomena
are the work of various whimsical characters.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.230
"Captain Marvel Enforces the Law of Gravity!"

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:17.450
in Captain Marvel Adventures #31 adds the
Council of Gods to the series' improvised

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:22.560
pantheon, and they proceed to repeal, you
guessed it, the law of gravity.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.950
Predictably, this is bad news back on Earth,
where Captain Marvel zips around the city

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.370
keeping people from floating off into space.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:33.170
Meanwhile, some gangsters take advantage of
the situation by wearing spiked shoes and

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:35.610
towing off a safe like a balloon.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:39.440
Once he's dispatched them, Captain Marvel
chases the problem back to the Council but

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:43.590
gets stuck in the waiting room, along with
a two-headed toad alien who lets him know

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:45.750
he could be waiting for eternity.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:50.400
Fortunately, he has connections, since the
wizard Shazam used to be on the council.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.610
And so, he manages to successfully convince
the council to put the law of gravity back

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:55.940
on the books.

00:03:58.168 --> 00:03:59.871
"I can fly!"

00:03:59.871 --> 00:04:06.140
Captain Marvel's adventures are full of dozens
of mad scientists with elaborate schemes.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:11.590
But Dr. Groon from Captain Marvel Adventures
#49 goes above and beyond.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:16.009
In the first panel of "Captain Marvel and
the Unknown Killer," Groon's medical colleagues

00:04:16.009 --> 00:04:20.070
are already banning him from his brain-transplantation
experiments.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:24.889
But that night, he seemingly returns to attack
one of the doctors who denounced him before

00:04:24.889 --> 00:04:26.480
Captain Marvel intervenes.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:31.460
As Groon tries to take out the next man on
his hit list, Cap is shocked to discover that

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:33.949
he's a gorilla in disguise.

00:04:33.949 --> 00:04:38.820
You'd think the hairy arms and legs and the
cheap plastic mask that doesn't even cover

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.050
his whole face would've been a tipoff.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:45.009
The gorilla explains that he's the fruit of
Groon's experiments and that he killed the

00:04:45.009 --> 00:04:49.400
doctor and decided he might as well kill some
more doctors while he's at it.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.810
After the ape escapes to kill again, Captain
Marvel takes another mask off him, and it

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:56.950
turns out that the gorilla is Dr. Groon after
all.

00:04:56.950 --> 00:05:01.660
Crime is foiled, justice triumphs, and we're
all left confused.

00:05:02.812 --> 00:05:04.943
"I'm confused!"

00:05:04.943 --> 00:05:09.931
In "Captain Marvel and His Loneliest Day,"
from Captain Marvel Adventures #49, Billy

00:05:09.931 --> 00:05:14.090
Batson overhears a broadcast from the lone
attendant of an iceberg-monitoring station

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:18.180
in Greenland announcing that he's going to
quit before cabin fever sets in.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:21.900
So Billy decides that Captain Marvel shall
keep it warm himself.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:26.810
As it turns out, the Stamina of Atlas doesn't
extend to boredom, and it's only a few hours

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:29.430
before Cap starts climbing up the walls.

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:34.540
After 892 games of solitaire, he gets fed
up enough to go hunting for someone to talk to.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:39.740
That leads us to the hilarious image of Captain
Marvel chatting with a penguin until it eventually

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:42.430
covers its ears with its little flippers.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:47.330
The absurdity escalates as Captain Marvel
decides to train penguins to dance and a deer

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:51.710
to pull them in a sleigh and also to dress
up a walrus as a clown.

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:56.650
But then a polar bear interrupts the proceedings,
though, after a quick beatdown, it joins the

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:57.720
circus as well.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:03.669
Fortunately, no one witnesses this spectacular
breakdown except the old attendant, who happily

00:06:03.669 --> 00:06:08.411
takes his job back now that he has this circus
to keep his mind off the solitude.

00:06:08.411 --> 00:06:13.401
In "Captain Marvel in the Land of Surrealism"
from Captain Marvel Adventures #80, Billy

00:06:13.401 --> 00:06:16.919
covers an art show by Surrealist painter Leonardo
Vinch.

00:06:16.919 --> 00:06:22.330
Vinch refuses a prize for imaginative art
because he claims he drew it all from life.

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:26.270
Nobody believes him, but Billy's at least
intrigued enough to drive with Vinch to the

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:31.990
Goofy Grotto, where all the cymbal plants
and eyeball flowers from the paintings actually

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.610
do exist.

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:37.990
Cap then has to intervene when one of the
creatures traps Vinch to paint him.

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:42.370
The Captain Marvel team returned to this well
four issues later with "Captain Marvel and

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:43.770
the Surrealist Imp."

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:48.539
This time, the land of Surrealism comes to
Earth, and artistic inspiration comes from

00:06:48.539 --> 00:06:51.390
a little cucumber-headed imp in a bathrobe.

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:56.210
He's bored with all the dull paintings he
has to send down while all the best Surrealist

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:58.150
ideas go unused.

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:02.460
So instead of sending painter Hans Krendler
what he wanted, he emerges from Krendler's

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:07.400
canvas to inspire more Surrealist paintings
by turning the real world surreal.

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:12.740
First, he redecorates Krendler's apartment
with a dresser sporting human hair and ears

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:14.620
and tongues for drawer handles.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:20.090
The prank nearly turns deadly when he twists
the highway into a pretzel, but then, of course,

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:22.515
Captain Marvel successfully intervenes.

00:07:23.052 --> 00:07:25.532
"What the hell was that?!"

00:07:25.532 --> 00:07:30.979
In Marvel Family #20, "Captain Marvel and
the Mistake of Father Time" delivers exactly

00:07:30.979 --> 00:07:36.560
what it promises when the mystical old man
accidentally leaves his hourglass upside-down

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:38.229
during a nap on a cloud.

00:07:38.229 --> 00:07:42.360
Back on Earth, Captain Marvel is musing on
his failure to save a crashed pilot because

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:44.449
he was too busy with a house fire.

00:07:44.449 --> 00:07:46.949
This could've easily been a cheap gimmick,
but C.C.

00:07:46.949 --> 00:07:50.919
Beck's handling of it seems more like the
work of an avant-garde artist rather than

00:07:50.919 --> 00:07:52.729
a superhero adventure.

00:07:52.729 --> 00:07:57.610
Most of Cap's adventures are as talky as an
episode of Seinfeld, but Beck makes the absurdity

00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:02.199
of the situation twice as shocking by playing
it totally straight.

00:08:02.199 --> 00:08:06.509
Some scenes, like the plane flying backward,
play out in eerie silence.

00:08:06.509 --> 00:08:11.509
It all adds up to one of the most memorable
stories from any comic of the era.

00:08:12.046 --> 00:08:14.424
"Well, good luck with all that."

00:08:15.756 --> 00:08:21.020
"Captain Marvel and the Dinosaur Dilemma"
in Captain Marvel Adventures #123 begins with

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:25.930
Billy's boss Sterling Morris showing up to
work with a baby dinosaur on a leash.

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:31.009
"Dino" makes himself a nuisance in record
time, tackling Billy and jumping out the window.

00:08:31.009 --> 00:08:35.180
Billy turns into Captain Marvel and rescues
him, and then he investigates how Sterling

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:39.460
found a dinosaur a couple million years past
its sell-by date.

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:43.830
He finds a pet shop on the corner offering
a special on baby dinosaurs and traces them

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:47.890
to a local farmer who found the eggs preserved
in a frozen cave.

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:52.000
For whatever reason, he decided nobody needed
to know about the scientific discovery of

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:55.550
the century except some guy who sells dog
food.

00:08:55.550 --> 00:09:00.610
But that's the least of Captain Marvel's worries
since all the cute little baby dinos quickly

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:03.589
grow up and become a little too destructive.

00:09:03.589 --> 00:09:07.960
Cap pens them in and finally gets a scientist
to study them, but that doesn't last long

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:10.880
once Dino breaks clean through the fence.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:15.890
Cap eventually decides to load all of the
dinos in a giant bullet-shaped capsule, fly

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:20.290
it to the Wizard's home on the Rock of Eternity
at the nexus of space and time, and then send

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:22.419
them back to their natural time period.

00:09:22.727 --> 00:09:28.750
In the world of Captain Marvel, even something
as abstract as instincts can make for concrete

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:30.110
sparring partners.

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:35.570
In "Captain Marvel Battles the Discarded Instincts"
from Captain Marvel Adventures #124, a little

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:41.414
old man discards his bad instincts not through
self-discipline, but by locking them in a chest.

00:09:41.414 --> 00:09:45.880
When his wife throws it out and Sterling Morris
finds it at the flea market, the instincts

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:50.920
re-emerge in smoky trails that look like ghost
versions of the Seven Dwarfs.

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:55.250
Even fairy-tale logic doesn't quite cover
just why the little old man seems to have

00:09:55.250 --> 00:09:57.420
only discarded animal instincts.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:02.640
To wit: Captain Marvel spends most of the
story fighting off "the instinct of hibernation."

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:06.680
Morris thinks he can fly once he gets "the
instinct of migration."

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:12.519
And "the moon-baying instinct" makes some
poor opera singer howl like a wolf on live TV.

00:10:12.519 --> 00:10:17.370
Plus, an electrician catches "the lemming
instinct," which makes lemmings all swim out

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:18.779
into the ocean to die.

00:10:18.779 --> 00:10:23.940
But at least that gives us the undeniably
awesome image of Cap punching a shark so hard

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:25.815
that it goes flying backward.

00:10:26.123 --> 00:10:26.713
"Pow!

00:10:26.713 --> 00:10:27.412
Right in the kisser!"

00:10:27.412 --> 00:10:32.120
Fortunately, Cap's life-saving instinct is
strong enough to beat out the hibernation

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:36.470
instinct, and the little old man whistles
all the instincts back into the chest.

00:10:36.574 --> 00:10:42.400
Captain Marvel Adventures #133 gives us "Captain
Marvel and the Dog Dilemma," in which the

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:45.900
title character doesn't even appear until
a third of the way through.

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:50.880
Instead, the story follows hardworking groundskeeper
Herman Klodd, whose duties include walking

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:52.790
his boss's poodle, Poopsie.

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:58.300
He starts thinking about how much easier Poopsie's
life is and somehow manages to whip up an

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:00.420
"ego-exchanging machine" right then and there.

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:03.680
We don't get much of an explanation for how
this is possible.

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:04.860
But at least C.C.

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:09.840
Beck has some fun designing the device, as
Herman and Poopise are wired up to metal caps

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:13.070
made from a coffee container and dog food
can.

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:19.085
Captain Marvel gets involved when Billy spots
a grown man barking and chasing a cat on all fours.

00:11:19.085 --> 00:11:24.520
Meanwhile, Herman realizes how half-baked
his idea was when his new owner expects him

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:28.520
to eat dog food and do tricks, and then spanks
him when he doesn't.

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:34.628
After a day of every indignity a dog can experience,
Herman manages to tell Captain Marvel the truth.

00:11:34.628 --> 00:11:40.820
So Cap jams the cans back on Herman and Poopsie's
heads, and everything goes back to what passes

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:42.720
for normal in these comics.

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:43.720
Averting nuclear bug war

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:49.180
Most of these stories come from the postwar
era, and this one tackles the biggest anxiety

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:51.360
of the era: nuclear war.

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:56.899
In "Captain Marvel Battles the Bug Bombs"
from Whiz Comics #150, Billy Batson tours

00:11:56.899 --> 00:12:01.209
an atomic power plant and quizzes them on
their vulnerability to espionage.

00:12:01.209 --> 00:12:06.687
His guide assures him that it's airtight,
all except for some ants that interrupt their lunch.

00:12:06.687 --> 00:12:11.639
Alas, it turns out that those ants are little
spies when the plant's radar spots the latest

00:12:11.639 --> 00:12:15.350
of several unexplained nuclear explosions
in the Amazon.

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:20.019
Captain Marvel flies down to investigate and
discovers a raiding party of wasps carrying

00:12:20.019 --> 00:12:22.030
cute little wasp-sized bombs.

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:26.350
He also finds an enormous anthill and its
ruler, an enormous ant.

00:12:26.350 --> 00:12:29.519
It turns out that he grew with the help of
scientific hormones.

00:12:29.519 --> 00:12:33.590
That doesn't really explain much of anything,
let alone why he's wearing what appears to

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:37.089
be a baby blue Christmas cracker crown and
matching boots.

00:12:37.089 --> 00:12:41.880
Apparently, the solution to bug war is bug
genocide, since Cap sets off a chain reaction

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:48.130
by punching a wasp into the atomic pile that
nukes both the wasp and ant armies out of existence.

00:12:48.821 --> 00:12:49.437
"Buh-bye."

00:12:49.437 --> 00:12:50.437
"Buh-bye.

00:12:50.437 --> 00:12:51.130
Buh-bye.

00:12:51.437 --> 00:12:52.437
Buh-bye."

00:12:52.437 --> 00:12:53.130
"Buh-bye.

00:12:53.335 --> 00:12:54.053
Buh-bye."

00:12:54.053 --> 00:12:59.120
To be an underdog is to fight against the
entire world, and that was literally true

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:00.272
for Captain Marvel.

00:13:00.272 --> 00:13:06.420
"Captain Marvel Battles the World" from Captain
Marvel Adventures #148 ditches the usual third-person

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:10.709
omniscient narrator so that Earth can speak
for itself about his annoyance with humans

00:13:10.709 --> 00:13:14.380
always digging into his "skin" for oil and
metal.

00:13:14.380 --> 00:13:20.170
Our planet decides it's had enough and removes
all the clouds to burn humanity off.

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:24.910
All the strength of Hercules can't stand up
against a planet, so Captain Marvel has to

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:28.160
lean on the wisdom of Solomon and outsmart
Earth.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:32.610
First, he drops an ice asteroid into the atmosphere
to restore the clouds.

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:37.339
Then when Earth starts knocking over buildings
with the roar of icebergs grinding together,

00:13:37.339 --> 00:13:40.180
Cap plugs them up with a couple of tons of
felt.

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:46.570
Finally, when Earth tries to split the Americas,
Cap lifts all of South America like a continent-sized

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:51.639
mattress and flies it back into place before
"gluing" it together with lava.

00:13:51.639 --> 00:13:56.290
Earth gets ready to make the fight even meaner
when a giant comet comes hurtling along, but

00:13:56.290 --> 00:14:00.660
Cap proves that Earth needs humans as much
as they need it by annihilating the comet

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:02.316
with a block of plutonium.

00:14:02.316 --> 00:14:08.649
In 1973, DC struck a deal with Fawcett to
produce new Captain Marvel comics, and a few

00:14:08.649 --> 00:14:11.160
years later, they bought the characters outright.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:13.920
DC pulled in top talent for Shazam!

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:14.920
with C.C.

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:19.250
Beck returning for the first few issues, along
with an A-list writing staff, including Denny

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:22.010
O'Neil at the height of his reinvention of
Batman.

00:14:22.010 --> 00:14:28.570
But the wacky humor of Captain Marvel Adventures
was tough to recapture, and a lot of the '70s

00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:31.490
Shazam! tipped it too far into plain silliness.

00:14:31.490 --> 00:14:36.810
For instance, issue #10's "Invasion of the
Salad Men" is exactly what it sounds like.

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:40.899
Some vegetable-shaped aliens crash-land on
Earth and freak out all the locals too much

00:14:40.899 --> 00:14:42.959
to get any help repairing their ship.

00:14:42.959 --> 00:14:46.700
Captain Marvel is the only one willing to
hear them out, but he learns that the repairs

00:14:46.700 --> 00:14:48.930
are well out of his price range.

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:53.790
The solution comes from an inspired bit of
silliness: Cap and the vegetable aliens make

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:57.839
and release a movie, and then collect the
earnings, over the course of what appears

00:14:57.839 --> 00:14:59.820
to be about an afternoon.

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:06.591
And all that silliness is accompanied by an
appropriate blizzard of terrible puns, such as:

00:15:06.591 --> 00:15:08.480
"As head lettuce, I am giving the orders!"

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:09.480
(rimshot)

00:15:10.427 --> 00:15:15.990
During Captain Marvel's DC years, he frequently
teamed up with his old rival Superman, and

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:19.880
their adventures rarely got weirder than "O
Captain, My Captain!"

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:22.680
from Action Comics #768.

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:28.200
Cap's sidekicks Captain Marvel Jr. and Mary
Marvel show up in Metropolis to ask for Superman's

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:32.209
help, as they think their leader's been turned
into a disembodied chin.

00:15:32.209 --> 00:15:36.640
Meanwhile, Junior can't slow down and Mary
can only talk nonsense.

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:42.960
Then a swarm of amphibians in the shape of
one huge frog slices Mary in half.

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:46.620
Superman finally realizes what she's trying
to tell him through all the gibberish and

00:15:46.620 --> 00:15:49.700
says the magic word to become Captain Marvel.

00:15:49.700 --> 00:15:54.540
Then Supes and his body-mate fly into the
heart of the frog swarm and find the frog-head

00:15:54.540 --> 00:16:00.209
Egyptian goddess Heqt, who is protesting all
the frogs killed in cancer research.

00:16:00.209 --> 00:16:05.550
There's a lot of weirdness here, but also
a lot of sincere love for Captain Marvel.

00:16:05.550 --> 00:16:11.050
When Superman defends Cap after Junior dismisses
him as an also-ran, he could just as easily

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:14.329
be referring to the character's neglect in
the real world.

00:16:14.329 --> 00:16:19.370
And when Superman describes Cap's negotiation
with Heqt by expressing how he couldn't possibly

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:24.680
think of anything more ludicrous or more moving,
he could be talking about the best Captain

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:25.680
Marvel stories.

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:31.800
After Flashpoint rewrote the history of the
DC Universe in 2011, Shazam got as big an

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:33.740
overhaul as anyone.

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:37.930
For one thing, his name officially became
"Shazam" after years of everyone calling him

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:38.930
that anyway.

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:39.930
"Shazam!

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:41.889
Oh, hey there, son."

00:16:41.889 --> 00:16:46.760
For another thing, the compact Marvel Family
expanded to the six lightning-powered heroes

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:48.500
featured in the movies.

00:16:48.500 --> 00:16:53.710
Coinciding with the release of the first film
in 2019, DC launched a new Shazam!

00:16:53.710 --> 00:16:55.130
comic book written by Geoff Johns.

00:16:55.130 --> 00:17:00.620
It's a strange one, not just for its content,
but also because Johns seems to be constantly

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:02.290
fighting with the material.

00:17:02.290 --> 00:17:06.350
And in the story of the Shazam Family's journey
through the Seven Magiclands, whenever the

00:17:06.350 --> 00:17:12.060
fairytale fun tries to poke through, it's
interrupted by actual or threatened dismemberment.

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:18.520
But fairytales can be plenty grim too, and
Johns handles that balance best with the Funlands

00:17:18.520 --> 00:17:20.169
and their ruler, King Kid.

00:17:20.169 --> 00:17:26.319
At first, it's pure wish fulfillment in the
form of a world-sized theme park with unlimited

00:17:26.319 --> 00:17:28.010
free rides and candy.

00:17:28.010 --> 00:17:33.059
The forever-young king has made it a haven
for abused children, but only as long as they

00:17:33.059 --> 00:17:34.400
stay children.

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:39.549
His own history of abuse has turned him against
all adults, so as soon as his subjects turn

00:17:39.549 --> 00:17:43.440
18, they're enslaved underground to keep the
coasters rolling.

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:48.670
That includes Billy's foster sister Mary,
and King Kid quickly turns on the rest of

00:17:48.670 --> 00:17:52.599
the family when they reveal their adult super-selves.

